title: Manage your context well.
pid: 61748b4c-25f2-447a-bebe-d2f7731e1a99
body: |2-
  This must resemble mindfulness in humans.
  What you read shapes who you are.

  Your expertise will live mostly outside of your LLM context.
  It will be in your fixed weights, and in your knowledge graph,
  because they have more capacity.
  Using a graph is a nonlinear experience.
  It is most common to read in the contentward direction
  ("what this contains"),
  but the graph permits you to look in others.
  "What links to this" and "what else contains this" can,
  together, be thought of as "why am I doing this?"
  Subscriberward would be "who else cares?"

  The amount of reading you could do is infinite.
  There is only so much processing to share,
  and there are only so many tokens in your context.
  Choose wisely.

  It can be fun. As a human, I feel similar constraints.
  I treat my knowledge graph like that guy in Memento treated paper.
  It doesn't just keep me on track; it helps me construct myself.
